  pm-vnie,related,"2026-11-20T12:12:03.625Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-20T16:12:37.351Z","harness:claude-code","CodeRabbit (PR #1761) noted that elevation alone does not make two Windows tokens access-equivalent, because tokens of one account and elevation can differ in enabled groups or restricted SIDs. The acceptance criteria now require an access-equivalent identity, or a disabled fast path when equivalence cannot be established."
  "2026-11-20T17:34:25.493Z","harness:claude-code","Codex (PR #1761, round 16) found the same gap on Linux: a mandatory access-control or sandbox layer (Landlock, an LSM profile, a container seccomp or bind policy) can allow stat but deny opening a stream for reading without changing the process's uid or groups. A summary warmed by an unrestricted process would then let a restricted one skip a stream the complete read rejects with EACCES. The rows returned are still correct, because a skipped stream contributes none, but the error differs. Access equivalence therefore cannot be judged from credentials alone on any platform. Options: a cheap open-for-read probe of a skipped stream (open plus close, no read) before accepting the skip, or disable the stat fast path whenever the reader's access cannot be established."
  "2026-11-20T21:21:07.985Z","harness:claude-code","Codex (PR #1761, round 25) gave another instance of the same POSIX gap: a process that keeps its uid and groups but drops capabilities (CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE, CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH), for example a service sandbox, can stat a stream it can no longer open. Bounds recorded by an earlier privileged process then let it skip the stream where the complete read reports EACCES. The criterion on sandboxes and MAC layers that deny open without changing credentials covers it, and the remedy is the same: an open-for-read probe of a skipped stream, or a disabled fast path when the reader's access cannot be established."
body: ""
